    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A salt-based water softener typically lasts 10 to 15 years, and a well-built one that is maintained can run 20 years or more, because the parts that wear out can be replaced separately. The resin bed usually lasts around 10 to 15 years on clean water but can be worn out in well under that by chlorine or iron, the control valve needs seals and sometimes a motor along the way, and the tanks themselves rarely fail. Here is what decides each lifespan, how to tell which part is failing, and when a rebuild beats a new system.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: 10 to 15 Years, Often Longer With Rebuilds
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ten to 15 years is the range most installers and manufacturers quote for a residential salt-based softener, and it is a fair expectation for a decent unit on municipal or reasonably clean well water. The number is less of a hard limit than it is for, say, a water heater, because nothing in a softener corrodes through and floods the basement. Parts wear, performance drops, and eventually someone decides it is not worth fixing.
          </p>

          <p>
            A softener has three main pieces. The mineral tank holds the resin beads that do the actual softening. The control valve on top runs the regeneration cycle and directs water through the resin. The brine tank holds the salt and makes the brine that recharges the resin. Each of those has its own lifespan, and the system lasts as long as you are willing to keep replacing whichever piece wears out first.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Lifespans</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10-15 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Whole softener, typical</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10-15 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Resin bed on clean, chlorine-free water</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20+ yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Fiberglass-reinforced mineral tank</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0-1 gpg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hardness a working softener should deliver</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That is why two softeners of the same age can be in completely different states. One on chlorinated city water with an aggressive regeneration schedule may have mushy resin and worn valve seals at year eight. Another on clean well water, sized properly and fed decent salt, can still be delivering zero-hardness water at year 20. The age on the label tells you less than the water it has been treating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salt-free conditioners are a different category with a different answer. They do not remove hardness; they change how minerals behave so they scale less. Their media is typically replaced every few years on the manufacturer's schedule, and the housing lasts much longer. Everything below is about true ion-exchange softeners, which is what our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best water softener systems</a> guide mostly covers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-part" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which Part of a Water Softener Wears Out First?
          </h2>

          <p>
            In most systems the first thing to need attention is the control valve, not the resin. Valves have pistons, seals, spacers and an injector that move or carry water every time the unit regenerates, and those soft parts wear. On electronic valves the motor, circuit board or water meter can fail too. The good news is that the most common valves are widely supported, and seal and piston kits are inexpensive.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Part</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical life</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What wears or fails</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Usually fixable?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Control valve</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10+ yrs with seal kits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seals, piston, injector, motor, board</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with parts or a new valve head</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Resin bed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-15 yrs, less with chlorine or iron</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oxidised, fouled or crushed beads</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, re-bedding</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mineral tank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20+ yrs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely fails; distributor tube can crack</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tube yes, tank rarely needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brine tank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Many years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brine valve, float, salt bridges and mush</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, cheap parts</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The resin is usually second. It wears gradually, losing capacity long before it stops working entirely, so the sign is a softener that runs out of softening between regenerations rather than one that fails overnight. How quickly it gets there depends almost entirely on what is in the water besides hardness, which is the subject of the next section.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tanks last longest. Mineral tanks on residential softeners are typically fiberglass-reinforced polyethylene, which does not rust, and they can outlast several resin beds. What can fail inside is the distributor tube, the riser pipe that runs down the middle with a screen at the bottom; a cracked tube or broken basket lets resin beads escape into the plumbing. The brine tank is a plastic bin with a float valve and is rarely the reason a system gets replaced.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is why the most useful question when a softener stops working is not how old it is, but which part has failed. A ten-year-old unit with a worn valve seal is a modest repair. A ten-year-old unit with exhausted resin is a re-bed. A ten-year-old unit with both, from a brand whose parts are hard to get, may be the one to replace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resin" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Long Does Water Softener Resin Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Softener resin is made of small polystyrene beads that hold sodium or potassium ions and trade them for calcium and magnesium as water passes through. Regeneration flushes the hardness off with brine and reloads the beads. Hardness on its own does not wear resin out; the beads can cycle through that exchange for many years. What shortens resin life is oxidation, fouling and physical damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chlorine is the main oxidiser. Municipal water carries chlorine or chloramine as a disinfectant, and over years it breaks down the cross-links that hold each bead together. The beads swell, soften and turn mushy, capacity falls and the bed starts to restrict flow, which shows up as low water pressure. On chlorinated water, standard 8 percent cross-linked resin often lasts around 10 years, while 10 percent cross-linked resin, designed to resist oxidation, typically lasts noticeably longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Iron is the main fouler. A softener can remove small amounts of dissolved iron along with hardness, but some of it stays behind and coats the beads, and oxidised iron or iron bacteria clog the bed outright. Manganese and sediment do similar damage. On chlorine-free well water with little iron, resin can reach 15 to 20 years; on water with iron and no pre-treatment, it may be fouled within a few years.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is that resin life is something you can influence. A carbon pre-filter ahead of the softener removes chlorine and protects the resin on city water. A dedicated iron filter, or at minimum regular use of a resin cleaner, protects it on iron-bearing well water. If your water has both chlorine and iron problems, the treatment ahead of the softener matters more to its lifespan than the softener itself. Our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best whole-home water filtration systems</a> guide covers those pre-filters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-shortens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Else Shortens a Softener's Life?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Undersizing is the most common design mistake. A softener that is too small for the household's water use and hardness regenerates every day or two, which means more cycles on the valve, more salt, more water down the drain and more wear on the resin. Many installers aim for a system that regenerates every several days to about a week. A unit that regenerates daily is often a unit that was chosen on price rather than on grain capacity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timer-based valves add wear of their own. A clock-controlled softener regenerates on a fixed schedule whether the resin is exhausted or not, so it cycles more often than it needs to in a light week. A metered, demand-initiated valve counts the gallons and regenerates only when the capacity is used up, which saves salt and spares the moving parts unnecessary cycles. If you are replacing a valve, metered is worth the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salt quality matters more than people assume. Rock salt is cheap but carries insoluble material that builds up as sludge in the brine tank and can foul the brine valve and injector. Evaporated salt pellets are the cleanest, and solar salt sits between them. Bridging, where a hard crust forms above the water with an empty space underneath, leaves the resin regenerating on weak brine and slowly losing capacity even though the salt bin looks full.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a softener set to the wrong hardness wears out faster or underperforms. Set it too low and hardness breaks through before regeneration; set it far too high and it regenerates more often than needed. Test your raw water hardness, add an allowance for iron if your manual calls for one, and set the valve to match. It takes five minutes and directly affects how many cycles the system goes through each year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Do You Know a Water Softener Is Failing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first sign is usually the return of hard water symptoms: white spots on glasses and fixtures, scale around faucets, soap that will not lather, a film on the shower door, or skin and hair that feel different. Dishwashers and water heaters suffer quietly in the meantime, which is one reason it pays to notice early; scale is part of what shortens the lives covered in <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long water heaters last</a> and <a href="/how-long-do-dishwashers-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long dishwashers last</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Confirm it with a test rather than a hunch. Hardness test strips are inexpensive: test water from a tap fed by the softener, then raw water from the bypass or an outdoor spigot that skips it. A working softener should deliver close to zero, typically 0 to 1 grain per gallon. If treated water reads close to raw, the softener is not softening, and the next job is working out why.
          </p>

          <p>
            The salt level is the quickest diagnostic. If the salt is not going down over several weeks, the unit is not drawing brine: check for a salt bridge, a clogged injector or brine line, or a valve that is not regenerating at all. If the brine tank is full of water well above the normal level, the brine valve or float is stuck, or the injector is blocked. Both are common and fixable problems rather than signs of a dead system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other symptoms point at specific parts. Small amber or brown beads in faucet aerators or the toilet tank mean resin is escaping, usually through a cracked distributor tube or broken bottom basket. A drop in water pressure that bypassing the softener cures points at mushy, chlorine-damaged or iron-fouled resin. Salty-tasting water usually means a brine draw or rinse problem. A blank display, wrong clock or a motor that hums without moving points at the valve's electronics.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more to rule out: a softener can harbour bacteria, particularly on well water or in a brine tank that has been neglected. If your water has started to smell of sulfur, test whether the smell is present before and after the softener. We cover that diagnosis in <a href="/why-does-my-water-smell-like-rotten-eggs/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">why your water smells like rotten eggs</a>, and sanitising the softener is often part of the cure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Should You Rebuild, Re-Bed or Replace a Softener?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Valve problems are usually repairs. A seal and spacer kit or a new piston for a widely used valve is a modest part and, on many valves, a manageable DIY job with the manual open. A failed motor or circuit board is more but still well under the cost of a new system. If the valve is an obscure proprietary design whose parts are no longer available, that changes the calculation, and a replacement valve head may be the answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Exhausted resin is a re-bed. You bypass the softener, remove the valve, empty the old resin, and refill with new resin and, usually, a layer of gravel or underbedding at the bottom. Resin is commonly sold by the cubic foot, often somewhere around $100 to $200 per cubic foot, and a typical household softener holds one to two cubic feet. Plumbers and water treatment dealers will do it if you would rather not handle 50-pound bags of wet beads.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacement makes sense when several things line up: the system is past about 15 years, both the resin and valve need work, parts are hard to find, or the unit was undersized in the first place. A new system resets all of those at once, and a correctly sized metered unit will usually use less salt and water than the old one. Prices run from under $1,000 for a capable DIY-installed system to considerably more for dealer-installed brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the size question as the tiebreaker. If your household has grown, your water usage has changed, or the softener regenerates nearly every day, putting new resin and seals into an undersized system means paying to keep a bad fit. If the system was sized correctly and the mineral tank is sound, a rebuild and re-bed is usually the better value. Our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">water softener rankings</a> explain how to size by grain capacity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Make a Water Softener Last Longer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Keep the salt right. Check the brine tank monthly, keep it roughly a quarter to half full, and keep the salt level a few inches above the water rather than packing it to the lid, which encourages bridging. Use clean pellets or solar salt rather than rock salt. Push a broom handle down through the salt now and then to break any crust, and clean out the brine tank when sludge or mush builds up at the bottom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the resin. On chlorinated city water, a whole-house carbon filter ahead of the softener removes the chlorine that attacks the beads. On well water with iron or manganese, follow the manufacturer's limits, use a resin cleaner on a regular schedule, and consider a dedicated iron filter if the levels are high. On water with sediment, a simple sediment pre-filter keeps grit out of the valve and resin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the settings once a year. Confirm the hardness setting still matches a fresh test of your raw water, that the clock is right after any power cut on timer models, and that regeneration happens at a time when nobody is using water, usually overnight. Glance at how often it regenerates; if that has crept up, either usage has changed or capacity is falling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat the softener as a system that is rebuilt rather than discarded. Keep the manual and the valve model number, buy the seal kit before you need it, and test treated water a couple of times a year. A softener looked after this way often goes well past 15 years, and every year it runs protects the water heater, dishwasher, fixtures and pipes downstream. If you want to compare what a new one would cost, our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best water softener systems</a> guide lays out the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing the whole system for a valve fault</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A softener that stops regenerating is very often a worn seal, a stuck piston, a clogged injector or a dead motor. All of those are parts. Before pricing a new system, find the valve model, check whether parts are available, and test whether the resin still softens after a manual regeneration. Many failing softeners need a repair, not replacement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring chlorine on city water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Municipal chlorine and chloramine slowly oxidise softener resin until it turns soft and restricts flow. Owners on city water who never pre-filter it often find their resin finished around year 10, or sooner. A carbon pre-filter costs far less than a re-bed and protects the resin, and you also get better-tasting water throughout the house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting salt bridges sit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hard crust of salt can form across the brine tank with empty space underneath, so the bin looks full while the resin regenerates on weak brine. Hardness slowly creeps back and owners assume the resin is dying. Check monthly, break up crusts with a broom handle, and avoid overfilling, especially in humid basements.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on price instead of grain capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An undersized softener regenerates every day or two, wasting salt and water and wearing out the valve and resin faster. Size it on your actual hardness, iron and daily usage, so it regenerates every several days to about a week. A slightly larger unit with a metered valve usually costs less to run and lasts longer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never testing the output</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many owners only notice a failed softener after the kettle, shower glass and water heater are already scaling up. Hardness test strips are cheap. Test treated water a couple of times a year and compare it with untreated water. A reading near zero means it is working; anything climbing toward raw means it needs attention now.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does water softener resin need to be replaced?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On chlorine-free water with little iron, resin commonly lasts 10 to 15 years and sometimes 20. On chlorinated city water, standard resin often wears out around year 10, and iron or manganese can foul it within a few years without pre-treatment. Replace it when a correctly set, regenerating softener no longer brings hardness close to zero, or when mushy resin restricts water pressure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you replace just the resin in a water softener?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Re-bedding means bypassing the softener, removing the control valve, emptying the old resin and refilling with new resin, usually with fresh gravel underbedding. It is a common job for plumbers and water treatment dealers and a reasonable DIY project if you can lift heavy bags. It costs far less than a new system when the tank and valve are still sound.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do water softeners wear out faster on well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily. Well water usually has no chlorine, which is the main thing that degrades resin, so softeners on clean well water can last longer than those on city water. The risk on wells is iron, manganese, sediment and bacteria, which foul the resin and valve. Test the well water and pre-treat for those, and a softener can last 20 years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much salt should be in a water softener?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most manufacturers suggest keeping the brine tank at least a quarter full and the salt a few inches above the water line. Filling it to the top in one go invites bridging, especially in a humid space. Check monthly, top up as needed, and choose clean pellets or solar salt, which leave less residue in the tank than rock salt.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my water softener using no salt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If salt is not going down, the softener is not drawing brine. The usual causes are a salt bridge, a clogged injector or brine line, a stuck brine valve, a timer reset by a power cut, or a valve that is not regenerating at all. Break up any crust, then start a manual regeneration and watch whether water is drawn out of the brine tank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth repairing a 15-year-old water softener?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often yes, if the mineral tank is sound, the valve is a widely supported design and the system was sized correctly. A seal kit, motor or re-bed costs a fraction of a new unit. If it needs both new resin and major valve work, uses an obsolete valve, or was always undersized, putting the money toward a new metered system usually makes more sense.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do salt-free water conditioners last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Salt-free conditioners do not use resin or regenerate, so there is no valve to wear out in the same way. The conditioning media gradually loses effectiveness and is typically replaced every few years, on a schedule set by the manufacturer and your water usage. The housing and fittings last much longer. Remember that they reduce scaling but do not actually soften water.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

